# Copy to .env.local. That file is gitignored.

# ─── Notion ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Required. The integration token that reads and writes the card database.
# Notion: Settings → Connections → your connection → Configuration →
# Integration token. The database id itself is not a secret and lives in
# lib/core/notion.ts; the token is what grants access to it.
#
# The database has to be shared with the connection individually
# (••• → Connections on the database), and the connection needs "Insert
# content" on top of read access or every write is refused while every read
# keeps working, which is the one failure that looks like a bug rather than a
# missing permission.
NOTION_TOKEN=

# ─── Writing ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Required for POST /v1/cards and GET /v1/fields. One shared passcode: one
# person edits this collection, so an account system with a password reset
# behind it would be furniture. Any long random string; `openssl rand -hex 24`.
#
# Without it every write answers 503 and says so, which is the intended state
# for a deployment nobody adds cards from.
CARDS_TOKEN=

# ─── Who may post ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Comma separated. Browsers on these origins may write; anything else is 403.
# A request with no Origin header at all is not a browser (the iOS app, curl)
# and is allowed: neither can be tricked into posting on someone else's behalf,
# which is the risk this defends against.
#
# Leave empty and no browser may write, including this app's own pages once
# they exist.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000
